# Testing — Kotlin pack

What to test and the four tiers: `testing.mdc`. This file is the Kotlin tooling.

- Runner: JUnit 6 (Jupiter) on the JVM, through Gradle (`./gradlew check`, or `./gradlew :usecases:test --tests '*NoteSync*'` for one; on `:app`, `:app:testDebugUnitTest`). Assertions from `kotlin.test`. `NoteTest.kt` in `src/test/kotlin/<same package>` beside `Note.kt`'s module. Names are sentences in backticks.
- **No test found is a failure.** Gradle 9 fails a test task whose sources compile to zero tests (a misnamed annotation, a class JUnit can't see), and `checkTestsRan` (in `check`) fails a module whose `LAYERS` row has `requiresTests` when it ran no tests, skipped (`@Disabled`) ones and an `@TestFactory` that makes none not counting (a test listener counts what ran). The gates refuse a row other than an adapter row (`ANY_LIBRARY`) without `requiresTests`, unless its `noTestsReason` says why. A module with no `requiresTests` and no test sources is skipped, not failed.
- Property tests with `kotest-property` (`checkAll(arb…) { … }`, standalone, no Kotest runner). Worked example: `domain/src/test/kotlin/com/example/domain/OutcomeTest.kt`. A failing run prints its seed; replay it with `checkAll(PropTestConfig(seed = <n>), …)` and keep that case as an example test.
- Fakes of ports live in `src/testFixtures/kotlin` (the `java-test-fixtures` plugin): off the production classpath, shared with any module's tests through `testImplementation(testFixtures(project(":usecases")))`. Assert the outcome (what the fake holds), not call counts.
- **Fault injection (tier 2) seed:** `usecases/src/test/kotlin/com/example/usecases/notesync/NoteSyncChaosTest.kt`, one test per fault kind plus a property over random fault scripts. The model server (`FaultServer`) and the fault-scripted outbox (`MemoryOutbox`) are `usecases/src/testFixtures/kotlin/com/example/usecases/chaos/`. Same fault kinds and the same script-by-request-index contract as the TypeScript seed. Virtual time is `runTest`: `delay` and `withTimeoutOrNull` run on its scheduler, so a 10-minute delay costs nothing; `currentTime` is the clock. The server's side of a slow request runs in `backgroundScope`, so a client timeout cancels the wait, not the server's late apply.
- **A kill is an exception** (`ProcessKilled`) the fake outbox throws at the scripted step; "restart" is a new `NoteSync` over the same `Disk`.
- Coverage and mutation testing: skipped, as in the TypeScript pack. Add one only for a named gap.
- **`:app`'s host tests** are JUnit 6 too (`app/src/test`, run by `testDebugUnitTest` in `check`, counted by `checkTestsRan`), over code with no Android types (`AppGraph`). AGP doesn't infer the JUnit flavour, so `app/build.gradle.kts` names `kotlin("test-junit5")`. No Robolectric (`docs/deferred.md` → the Android leg's open ends).
- **Instrumented tests are a separate leg** (`app/src/androidTest`: JUnit 4 and `androidx.test`, which is what runs on the device; names are snake_case sentences, since a method name with spaces doesn't dex below API 30). `pnpm kotlin:device` runs them on whatever emulator or device adb sees, never in `pnpm check`. A device is machine-wide, so it takes a lock in the OS temp directory and exits 75 when another run holds it: wait or skip, never kill it. CI runs them in the "Android tests (emulator)" job (ready PRs only). Keep logic in `:domain`/`:usecases`, so the host-only JVM tests reach it.
- **The gates skip locally when nothing they read changed.** `pnpm kotlin:gates` (~40 s) hashes every `*.gradle.kts` / `*.gradle` and `gradle.properties` in the tree, all of `gradle/` (wrapper jar and properties, catalog, applied scripts), all of `buildSrc/` and each included build, `detekt.yml`, `stacks/kotlin/gates*.mjs`, and the list of source package directories; after a pass it writes the hash taken at the start into `build/kotlin-gates.stamp`, and writes nothing if the tree changed mid-run. The next local run with the same hash prints one line and skips. Editing a module's `.kt` file doesn't invalidate it (a probe plants its own code); build logic, a new package, or `CI` set (CI always) runs them. Delete the stamp to force a run. Tested in `stacks/kotlin/gates.test.mjs`.
- Don't pipe `./gradlew` through `head`/`grep`: the pipe hides the exit code (`testing.mdc`).
